## Performance Considerations

### Theory: Response Time Analysis

```
Typical CICS Transaction Breakdown:

Total Response Time: ~500ms

├─ Terminal I/O          100ms (20%)
│  └─ User input display
│
├─ Program Execution     150ms (30%)
│  └─ Business logic
│  └─ Calculations
│
├─ Database I/O          200ms (40%)
│  └─ VSAM reads
│  └─ DB2 queries
│
└─ Network              50ms (10%)
   └─ Transmission
   └─ Protocol overhead
```

### Optimization Strategies

#### 1. Minimize Database Calls

```cobol
* INEFFICIENT: Multiple reads for same data
PERFORM VARYING INDEX 1 BY 1 UNTIL INDEX > 100
    EXEC CICS READ
        DATASET('MASTERS')
        INTO(MASTER-RECORD)
        RIDFLD(MASTER-KEY)
    END-EXEC
    * 100 separate I/Os
END-PERFORM.

* EFFICIENT: Read once, use multiple times
EXEC CICS READ
    DATASET('MASTERS')
    INTO(MASTER-RECORD)
    RIDFLD(MASTER-KEY)
END-EXEC.

PERFORM VARYING INDEX 1 BY 1 UNTIL INDEX > 100
    * Use MASTER-RECORD from storage
    PERFORM PROCESS-WITH-CACHE
END-PERFORM.
```

#### 2. Caching Strategy

```
Three-Level Cache:

Level 1: Application Cache
├─ In-program storage
├─ Access time: < 1ms
├─ Size: Limited by program size

Level 2: Temporary Storage
├─ CICS-managed cache
├─ Access time: 10-50ms
├─ Size: Larger, configurable

Level 3: Database
├─ VSAM/DB2
├─ Access time: 100-500ms
└─ Primary storage
```

#### 3. Connection Pooling

```
Without Pooling:
Request 1 → Create connection → Query → Close
Request 2 → Create connection → Query → Close
(High overhead, many connections)

With Pooling:
Connection Pool (Pre-allocated)
├─ Connection 1 → Ready
├─ Connection 2 → Ready
└─ Connection 3 → Ready

Request 1 → Get Connection 1 → Query → Release
Request 2 → Get Connection 2 → Query → Release
(Reuse, minimal overhead)
```

